<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>25: Laplace's equation on a cylinder</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Today we're going to look at a final example just to see how
everything fits together.  Let's consider Laplace's equation
<m>\nabla^2 u = 0</m> in a cylinder of radius <m>R</m> and height <m>H</m> where we
require <m>u=0</m> on the top and bottoms ``lids'' and allow <m>u</m> to be
possibly non-zero on the curvy side.</p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>State the boundary conditions carefully.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Now we're going to separate variables so let's try
  <md>
  u(r,\theta,z) = S(r,\theta) Z(z).
  </md></p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>What differential equations do we find with separation of variables?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>How do the boundary conditions translate into conditions on either
  <m>S</m> or <m>Z</m>?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>Where is the Sturm-Liouville problem?  Is this a familiar
  Sturm-Liouville problem?  Find the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>Let's move on the other equation.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>How will we solve it (hint:
  separation of variables)?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>Where is the Sturm-Liouville problem?  Is this a familiar
  Sturm-Liouville problem?  Find the eigenvalues and eigenfunctions.</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>There is one final differential equation for us to solve.  Is this a
  familiar equation?</p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Find the solutions to this equation that are bounded in the
  cylinder.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="3in">
    <statement>
      <p>Put everything together to write the general solution of the
  equation.</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="1in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose we know that <m>u(R,\theta,z) = f(\theta, z)</m>.  How would
  we find the solution?</p>
    </statement>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
